{"name":"Centipede Brown","hex":"#6d2400","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"centipede","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any arthropod of class Chilopoda, which have a segmented body with one pair of legs per segment and from about 20 to 300 legs in total.","A many-oared Chinese smuggling boat."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/centipede"},{"term":"brown","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A colour like that of chocolate or coffee.","One of the colour balls used in snooker, with a value of 4 points."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a brown color/colour.","Gloomy."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/brown"}],"entries":[{"title":"Centipede","blocks":[{"text":"Centipedes are predatory arthropods belonging to the class Chilopoda of the subphylum Myriapoda, an arthropod group which includes millipedes and other multi-legged animals. Centipedes are elongated segmented (metameric) animals with one pair of legs per body segment. All centipedes are venomous and can inflict painful stings, injecting their venom through pincer-like appendages known as forcipules or toxicognaths, which are actually modified front legs instead of fangs. Despite the name, no species of centipede has exactly 100 legs; the number of pairs of legs is an odd number that ranges from 15 pairs to 191 pairs. Less common names include \"forty-legs\" and \"hundred-legs\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/Chilopoda_collage.png/330px-Chilopoda_collage.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Centipede","wikidata":"Q43447"},{"title":"Brown","blocks":[{"text":"Brown is a color. It can be thought as a darker, typically desaturated shade of orange, and can often be produced by combining red and yellow with another color, namely blue."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Espresso-roasted_coffee_beans.jpg/330px-Espresso-roasted_coffee_beans.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brown","wikidata":"Q47071"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/Eupolybothrus_cavernicolus_forcipules.jpg/500px-Eupolybothrus_cavernicolus_forcipules.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/Eupolybothrus_cavernicolus_forcipules.jpg/1280px-Eupolybothrus_cavernicolus_forcipules.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/Eupolybothrus_cavernicolus_forcipules.jpg/500px-Eupolybothrus_cavernicolus_forcipules.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The forcipules of Eupolybothrus cavernicolus (Lithobiidae)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEupolybothrus_cavernicolus_forcipules.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Centipede_ultimate_legs_collage.jpg/500px-Centipede_ultimate_legs_collage.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Centipede_ultimate_legs_collage.jpg/1280px-Centipede_ultimate_legs_collage.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Centipede_ultimate_legs_collage.jpg/500px-Centipede_ultimate_legs_collage.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A collage showing the ultimate legs of various centipedes. From top left, proceeding clockwise: Rhysida spp., Scolopocryptops trogloclaudatus, Scolopendra dehaani, Lithobius proximus, Lithobius forficatus, Scolopendra cingulata.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACentipede_ultimate_legs_collage.jpg"},{